  "query_preview": "..Upper level Low is moving into the state this\nafternoon. This will continue the shower chances this afternoon, \nbut the 18Z sounding is already showing some significant drying in\nthe mid and upper levels of the atmosphere so additional rainfall\namounts will be lower than the last few days. By this evening and\novernight hours showers will end over the western half of the \narea, but will linger along the Utah/Arizona border and the far \neastern part of the state through Wednesday afternoon. Not only \nwill this Low bring some drier air but tonight could see some of \nthe coldest if not the coldest readings so far this Fall. Could\neven see some snow mix in briefly at elevations above 7000 ft\nbefore these showers move east.\n\nBy Wednesday evening this Low will have pushed east and things\nfinally dry out for Thursday and Friday. As the trough pushes east\nit takes a positive tilt and will spin up a cutoff Low to the\nsouthwest of the state by the weekend. At the same time a ridge\nmoves in over the West forming a Rex block pattern. Winds from the\neast and northeast will be funneled between this ridge and the\ncutoff Low making for a breezy Thursday and Friday with winds\nremaining elevated Thursday night. Didn't issue an Advisories\nduring this shift but future shifts will have to monitor closely\nto see if a Wind Advisory is warranted Thursday into Friday.\n\nAs the Low settles to our southwest, it may be able to tap some\nsubtropical moisture, possibly from Tropical Cyclone Simon. If\nthis happens the higher elevations could see some showers move in\nFriday night, Saturday and into midday Sunday. The rainfall\npotential from this scenario does not look to be the magnitude of\nthe last event at this time. However cutoff Lows are famously\ntough to forecast for and the subtropical moisture tap may not be\nrealized so this remains a very fluid forecast. Will just have to\nkeep monitoring and see how things start coming together in the\nupcoming model runs.",
